ps5/hdmi: restore video output after an EDID notification

The PS5's HDMI port is driven by a FLAVA3 transmitter behind an internal DP
link. When the TV re-handshakes HDMI -- switched on, woken, or switched back to
this input -- the ICC firmware sends an EDID notification and
hdmi_notification_handler() calls sceHdmiOutputMode(), which disturbs the
transmitter's video path and leaves the output muted.

DRM is never told. There is no connector event and no atomic commit, so
amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() does not run and nothing calls
sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(0). The GPU carries on scanning out a valid picture
into a chip that discards it while still driving sync, so the TV reports a live
signal and shows black. Every register the kernel can read is identical to a
working display -- a fault capture diffed against a healthy baseline shows no
meaningful difference -- because every transmitter call is a write-only I2C
poke with no readback. Recovering needs a full modeset, which in practice means
SSHing in.

This is the "screen save does not work properly" entry in the
ps5-linux-patches README: the console appears to have blanked and cannot be
woken from the couch, but it is not a screensaver or a DPMS state at all.

Restore the output from the handler instead, replaying what amdgpu last
programmed. This touches only the transmitter -- no modeset, no DRM
involvement, no dependency on a compositor being alive -- so it works in Steam
Game Mode (gamescope, which has no D-Bus API to force a modeset) and at the
display-manager greeter alike.

amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() brings the transmitter up in two halves, either
side of committing the streams. First it mutes and calls
sceHdmiInitVideoConfig(); afterwards it programs the mode, unmutes, and sets
audio up. Replaying only the second half is not enough. It does rebuild the
HDMI output PHY, since setHdmiVideoConfigFlava3() covers link training and both
PHY init steps, but nothing then restores what sceHdmiInitVideoConfig() owns:
the encode teardown handshake in sceDisableEncode(), the ISR registers, and
initDpForFlava3(), which sets up the DP *receiver* facing the GPU.
sceHdmiOutputMode() disturbs exactly those. After a second-half-only restore
the chip drives sync and plays audio -- a separate path with its own mute --
while passing no video, which presents identically to no restore at all.

Replay both halves. The GPU streams throughout and is unaware any of this
happened, so the re-init retrains against a live signal rather than a dark
link.

Because none of this reads back, it cannot be determined statically how much of
the sequence is really required, and the useful amount has already been
mis-estimated once. The behaviour is therefore selectable at runtime via
hdmi.restore_output:

  0 - do nothing (pre-fix behaviour)
  1 - unmute only (cheapest; does not retrain the internal DP link)
  2 - replay the video+audio config amdgpu last programmed
  3 - re-init the transmitter first, then replay it (default)

Also serialise the exported transmitter entry points. They all build their ICC
message in the single global i2c_ctx with no locking; an EDID notification
arriving while amdgpu is mid-commit could already interleave two message builds
and send a malformed packet, and this change lengthens the handler's I2C
activity enough to make that materially more likely.

Not fixed here: hdmi_notification_handler() leaks the previous real_edid on
every notification. Freeing it would risk a use-after-free, since amdgpu reads
that pointer without any synchronisation against this path -- the leak is the
safer of the two bugs and wants a separate fix.

Tested on a PS5 (Oberon) running kernel 7.1.3 with a 4K TV over the internal
DP -> FLAVA3 path. Powering the TV off and on, or switching it to another input
and back, reproduces the blackout with restore_output=0 and restores the
picture within about a second at the default, logging:

  hdmi: got real edid
  hdmi: restored hdmi output (3840x2160, 8 ch, restore_output=3)

Early in boot, before amdgpu has committed a mode, there is nothing cached to
replay and the handler falls back to unmuting alone, logging "(unmute only, no
mode cached)".

History worth recording, since it is the reason mode 3 exists: mode 2 was the
original default and appeared to work for days under GNOME. It was not the
thing fixing the screen. Mutter reacts to the connector event with a genuine
atomic modeset, which runs the whole sequence through amdgpu and would have
recovered the picture with no patch at all. Under gamescope, which does not
remodeset on hotplug, mode 2 was left on its own and the blackout came back --
with audio, which is what pointed at the missing half.

--- a/drivers/ps5/hdmi.c
+++ b/drivers/ps5/hdmi.c
@@ -3,8 +3,10 @@
 #define pr_fmt(fmt) KBUILD_MODNAME ": " fmt
 
 #include <linux/module.h>
+#include <linux/mutex.h>
 #include <linux/ps5.h>
 #include <drm/drm_edid.h>
+#include <drm/drm_modes.h>
 
 #define HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3	2
 #define HDMI_IC_TYPE_VERDE	3
@@ -102,6 +104,40 @@
 
 static struct i2c_context i2c_ctx;
 
+/*
+ * Serialises the transmitter sequences below. They all build their ICC
+ * message in the single global i2c_ctx, so an EDID notification arriving
+ * while amdgpu is mid-commit would interleave two message builds and send a
+ * malformed packet.
+ *
+ * Only the exported sceHdmi* entry points and hdmiSystemResume() take this
+ * lock, and none of them calls another locked function, so it never nests.
+ */
+static DEFINE_MUTEX(hdmi_lock);
+
+/*
+ * Last video/audio configuration amdgpu programmed, replayed by
+ * hdmi_notification_handler() to bring the output back after the ICC
+ * firmware reprograms the transmitter behind DRM's back.
+ */
+static struct drm_display_mode last_mode;
+static bool last_mode_valid;
+static int last_channels = 2;
+
+/*
+ * How hdmi_notification_handler() restores output after an EDID
+ * notification:
+ *   0 - do nothing (pre-fix behaviour: the screen stays black)
+ *   1 - unmute only (cheapest, does not retrain the internal DP link)
+ *   2 - replay the video+audio config amdgpu last programmed
+ *   3 - additionally re-init the transmitter first, i.e. replay the whole
+ *       sequence amdgpu runs across a modeset (default)
+ */
+static int restore_output = 3;
+module_param(restore_output, int, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(restore_output,
+	"Restore HDMI output after an EDID notification: 0=off, 1=unmute only, 2=replay video+audio config, 3=re-init transmitter then replay (default 3)");
+
 static u8 hdmi_ic_type;
 static u8 tmds_polarity;
 static u8 tmds_ch_swap;
@@ -522,6 +558,7 @@
 
 void hdmiSystemResume(void)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
 	// sceSetBackToUnpluggedSequence();
 	// sceSetBackToWaitResolutionSequence();
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
@@ -537,6 +574,7 @@
 		unk_akv();
 	}
 	sceControlHdmiEvent(1);
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(hdmiSystemResume);
 
@@ -1093,6 +1131,7 @@
 
 void sceHdmiInitVideoConfig(void)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
 	stopHdcpHw();
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
 		sceDisableEncode();
@@ -1102,21 +1141,27 @@
 	} else {
 		initVerde();
 	}
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(sceHdmiInitVideoConfig);
 
 void sceHdmiSetVideoConfig(const struct drm_display_mode *mode)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
+	drm_mode_copy(&last_mode, mode);
+	last_mode_valid = true;
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
 		setHdmiVideoConfigFlava3(mode);
 	} else {
 		setHdmiVideoConfigVerde(mode);
 	}
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(sceHdmiSetVideoConfig);
 
 void sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(int mute)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
 	i2c_init(4);
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
 		i2c_write(0x705f, (mute == 1) << 7);
@@ -1124,21 +1169,26 @@
 		i2c_mask(0x105, mute != 1, 0x03);
 	}
 	i2c_exec();
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute);
 
 void sceHdmiSetAudioConfig(int channels)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
+	last_channels = channels;
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
 		sceHdmiSetAudioConfigFlava3(channels);
 	} else {
 		sceHdmiSetAudioConfigVerde(channels);
 	}
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(sceHdmiSetAudioConfig);
 
 void sceHdmiSetAudioMute(int mute)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
 	i2c_init(4);
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
 		i2c_mask(0x70a8, (mute == 1) << 2, 0x04);
@@ -1146,16 +1196,19 @@
 		i2c_cmd_5_5(mute == 1);
 	}
 	i2c_exec();
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(sceHdmiSetAudioMute);
 
 void sceHdmiOutputMode(void)
 {
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
 	i2c_init(4);
 	if (hdmi_ic_type == HDMI_IC_TYPE_FLAVA3) {
 		i2c_write(0x7005, 0x80);
 	}
 	i2c_exec();
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(sceHdmiOutputMode);
 
@@ -1229,11 +1282,84 @@
 
 void hdmi_notification_handler(struct icc_msg *msg)
 {
-		if (msg->data[1] == 0x02) {
-			fix_edid(&msg->data[4]);
-			real_edid = drm_edid_alloc(&msg->data[4], *(u16 *)&msg->data[2]);
-			pr_info("got real edid\n");
-			sceHdmiOutputMode();
-		}
+	struct drm_display_mode mode = {};
+	bool have_mode;
+	int channels;
+
+	if (msg->data[1] != 0x02)
+		return;
+
+	fix_edid(&msg->data[4]);
+	real_edid = drm_edid_alloc(&msg->data[4], *(u16 *)&msg->data[2]);
+	pr_info("got real edid\n");
+	sceHdmiOutputMode();
+
+	/*
+	 * sceHdmiOutputMode() has just reprogrammed the transmitter, which
+	 * leaves video muted -- and DRM is never told. There is no connector
+	 * event and no atomic commit, so amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() does
+	 * not run and nothing calls sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(0). The GPU
+	 * keeps scanning out a valid picture into a chip that discards it
+	 * while still driving sync, so the TV reports a live signal and shows
+	 * black until something forces a modeset.
+	 *
+	 * Restore the output here instead. This touches only the transmitter:
+	 * no modeset, no DRM involvement, and no dependency on a compositor
+	 * being alive -- so it works in Game Mode and at the greeter alike.
+	 */
+	if (restore_output <= 0)
+		return;
+
+	if (restore_output == 1) {
+		sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(0);
+		pr_info("restored hdmi output (unmute only)\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
+	mutex_lock(&hdmi_lock);
+	have_mode = last_mode_valid;
+	if (have_mode)
+		drm_mode_copy(&mode, &last_mode);
+	channels = last_channels;
+	mutex_unlock(&hdmi_lock);
+
+	if (!have_mode) {
+		/* Nothing programmed yet (early boot): unmute is all we can do. */
+		sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(0);
+		pr_info("restored hdmi output (unmute only, no mode cached)\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * amdgpu_dm_atomic_commit_tail() brings the transmitter up in two
+	 * halves, either side of committing the streams: it mutes and calls
+	 * sceHdmiInitVideoConfig(), then programs the mode and unmutes.
+	 *
+	 * Mode 2 replays only the second half. That rebuilds the HDMI output
+	 * PHY -- setHdmiVideoConfigFlava3() does link training and both PHY
+	 * init steps -- but nothing restores the state sceHdmiInitVideoConfig()
+	 * owns: the encode teardown handshake in sceDisableEncode(), the ISR
+	 * registers, and initDpForFlava3(), which sets up the *DP receiver*
+	 * facing the GPU. sceHdmiOutputMode() disturbs those, so after a mode-2
+	 * restore the chip drives sync and plays audio (a separate path, with
+	 * its own mute) while passing no video: the TV reports a live signal
+	 * and shows black, exactly as it does with no restore at all.
+	 *
+	 * Mode 3 replays both halves. The GPU is streaming throughout -- we
+	 * never stop it, and it is unaware any of this happened -- so the
+	 * re-init retrains against a live signal rather than a dark link.
+	 */
+	if (restore_output >= 3) {
+		sceHdmiSetAudioMute(1);
+		sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(1);
+		sceHdmiInitVideoConfig();
+	}
+
+	sceHdmiSetVideoConfig(&mode);
+	sceHdmiDeviceSetVideoMute(0);
+	sceHdmiSetAudioConfig(channels);
+	sceHdmiSetAudioMute(0);
+	pr_info("restored hdmi output (%s, %d ch, restore_output=%d)\n",
+		mode.name, channels, restore_output);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(hdmi_notification_handler);
